.TH RCMD 1
.SH NAME
rcmd \- remote command execution
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rcmd
[
.B -1
] [
.B -A
] [
.B -a
.I cryptoalg
] [
.B -e
.I exportpath
] [
.B -f
.I keyfile
]
.I host
[
.I cmd
.I arg ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Rcmd
executes
.I cmd
on the given
.IR host .
If no
.I cmd
is given,
.IR sh (1)
is assumed.
The
.I host
must have enabled the
.L rstyx
service (typically started via
.IR svc (8)).
.PP
The default version 2 request waits for an acknowledgment from
.IR rstyxd (8)
before exporting caller files.  A server that rejects the transport or command
therefore gives the command a failing status instead of an ambiguous empty
success.  The
.B -1
option selects the original unacknowledged protocol for compatibility with an
old server; its status cannot report early server rejection reliably.
The acknowledgment covers request acceptance, not the eventual exit status of
the remote program.
.PP
For authentication,
.I rcmd
will use the certificate in the file
.IP
.BI /usr/ username /keyring/ net ! machine
.PP
if it exists, and otherwise it will use the certificate in
.IP
.BI /usr/ username /keyring/default .
.PP
The
.B -a
option sets the algorithm
.I cryptoalg
to be used following authentication for digesting or encryption.
See 
.IR ssl (3)
for the supported algorithms.
The default is AES-256-CBC with SHA-256, providing confidentiality and
integrity after authentication.  The
.B -A
option disables authentication and record protection explicitly.
.PP
The
.B -e
option sets the path to be exported as root from the local machine.  By
default, a private synthetic root containing only the caller's
.B /dev
is exported.  Use
.B -e /
only when the remote command deliberately needs the caller's full namespace.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/rcmd.b
